================================================================================
ZZ/p ARing classes: coverage baseline and audit notes
================================================================================

Working notes for the ZZ/p unit-test effort on branch 'arings'.  Scope is the
three ZZ/p ARing implementations only; Galois fields are out of scope here.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Coverage baseline
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Measured 2026-09-14, before any ZZ/p unit-test changes.  Recorded so later work
can be measured against a known starting point.

How to reproduce:

  cmake -DGCOV=ON ... <other flags>          # see BUILD/anton/Makefile, target cmake-gcov
  cmake --build . --target M2-unit-tests
  cmake --build . --target coverage-reset    # clear accumulated .gcda counters
  ./Macaulay2/e/M2-unit-tests                # 277 tests, all passing at baseline
  gcovr --root <srcdir> Macaulay2/e/CMakeFiles \
        --merge-mode-functions=merge-use-line-min \
        --gcov-ignore-errors=all --filter '.*aring-ZZp.*' --txt

Point gcovr at Macaulay2/e/CMakeFiles rather than at just the M2-engine object
directory.  Most of each ARing class is inline code in the .hpp, which gcov
attributes to whichever translation unit instantiated it -- including the
unit-test TUs.  Narrowing the search to the engine objects alone makes the
headers report a spuriously low line count at 0%.

"base" is the starting point.  "gen" adds the generator, discreteLog and
modulus-range tests.  "matrix" adds the typed test matrix (section 2).  "ops"
adds the strengthened helpers and the element-operation tests (section 3).

Note the test count falls from 283 to 268 at the matrix step while coverage
rises: eighteen hand-written per-modulus tests collapse into three typed ones
that each cover more ground.  Final count is 273, plus 2 disabled.

  File                  Lines    base      gen     matrix     ops
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  aring-ZZp.cpp            49   45 91%   45 91%   45 91%    45 91%
  aring-ZZp.hpp           150   83 56%   83 56%   90 61%   141 94%
  aring-ZZp-ffpack.cpp    143   79 55%   91 63%   96 67%   119 83%
  aring-ZZp-ffpack.hpp     35   14 40%   23 65%   23 65%    26 74%
  aring-ZZp-flint.cpp      35   26 74%   31 88%   31 88%    31 88%
  aring-ZZp-flint.hpp     105   72 68%   73 69%   79 75%    94 89%
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  TOTAL                   517  319 62%  346 67%  364 70%   456 88%

Still uncovered, and why:

  - promote / lift / eval (ring maps) in all three.  These take a Ring* or a
    RingMap*, so they cannot be reached from an ARing-level test.
  - ARingZZp::syzygy, ARingZZp::random.
  - ARingZZpFFPACK::field().


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. The test matrix
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ARingZZpTest.cpp drives all three classes through one TYPED_TEST over one list
of moduli, from p=2 up to the largest prime below 2^64.  ARingFactory<RT>
supplies make() and supports(); supports() is the single place each class's
ceiling is written down, and cells that fall outside it are printed with the
reason rather than being silently absent, which is how they were expressed
before.

This replaced eighteen per-modulus tests.  Those had drifted: ffpack never ran
above 32767 or at 32771, only some called testCoerceToLongInteger, and every
large-characteristic case hand-rolled a copy of testFiniteField with testPower
left out.

testPower was what forced that duplication -- it held the cardinality in an
int, which truncates above 2^31.  It now builds an mpz and calls power_mpz for
the q and q-1 exponents, keeping power() for the small ones (-1, -2, 3).  So
testPower runs at every modulus now, including 2^63 and 2^64 for flint, and
power_mpz has coverage in all three classes for the first time.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Element operations, and two API traps
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

testMultiply used to check only a*0 == 0 (it said so in its own TODO); it now
also covers a*1, agreement with repeated addition, and a*(-b) == -(a*b).
testAxioms gained the identities, right distributivity and a-a == 0.  testPower
gained a^0, 1^n, a^2 and a^3.  testFieldDivideByZero is new: divide(a,0) and
invert(0) must throw, 0/a == 0, a/a == 1, and every nonzero element is a unit.
There was previously no EXPECT_THROW anywhere in the aring harness.

Two things to know before writing more of these:

  - set(c, a) is not a copy.  Where ElementType is an integral type -- int for
    ARingZZp, double for ffpack -- passing an element to set() binds to the
    integer-coercion overload and reinterprets the element's internal value.
    Use init_set.  copy() exists on ARingZZp and ARingZZpFFPACK but not on
    ARingZZpFlint, so init_set is the only copy the three have in common.

  - ARingZZp::subtract_multiple requires nonzero arguments.  Elements are
    exponents of a primitive root, with 0 reserved for the field's zero, so
    zero has no valid exponent and needs a special case.  mult() has it;
    subtract_multiple() drops it for speed (the comment there cites a 15-20%
    gain from mimicking coeffrings.cpp) and documents "we assume: a, b are
    NONZERO!!".  Nothing checks it -- there is no assert in the whole file --
    so a zero operand silently yields a wrong answer rather than failing.  It
    stays in bounds, so it is not a memory-safety issue.  ffpack and flint
    store ordinary residues and have no such precondition.
    Recorded as ARingZZp.DISABLED_subtractMultipleByZero.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. The three classes differ at the core
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

They are not variations on one representation:

  ARingZZp         discrete log -- an element is the *exponent* of a primitive
                   root, stored as int, backed by log_table/exp_table.
                   0 -> 0, p-1 -> 1.  Strategy => "Aring".
  ARingZZpFFPACK   residue as a double, via Givaro::Modular<double>.
                   Strategy => "Ffpack".
  ARingZZpFlint    residue 0..p-1 as mp_limb_t, via flint nmod.
                   The default strategy for ZZ/p.

Legacy non-ARing counterpart: Z_mod (rings/ZZp.hpp), Strategy => "Old".

Both ARingZZp and ARingZZpFlint compute a primitive root eagerly in the
constructor; ARingZZpFFPACK computes its generator lazily.  Since Flint is the
default, every ZZ/p ring creation currently pays a factorization of p-1
(n_primitive_root_prime), whether or not a generator is ever used.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. Modulus limits -- three unrelated causes
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  ARingZZp        cap 32749, enforced at interface/aring.cpp:43.
                  Reason: two newarray_atomic(int, p) tables, plus an O(p^2)
                  primitive-root search in findPrimitiveRoot.  A real limit.

  ARingZZpFFPACK  native 94906266, enforced 32766.
                  Givaro's Modular<double>::maxCardinality() is 94906266 =
                  floor(2^26*sqrt(2) + 1/2), because Compute_t must hold p(p-1)
                  exactly and a double has a 53-bit mantissa.
                  But getMaxModulus() returns a hardcoded 0x7fff behind #if 1,
                  commented "I have no idea what value would be correct here",
                  with the real Givaro query stranded in the dead #else.
                  This is not cosmetic: interface/aring.cpp:165 gates ring
                  creation on it, so Strategy => "Ffpack" errors out above
                  p = 32766 despite the backend handling 94.9 million.
                  Corroboration that 32767 is wrong: the class's own
                  to_ring_elem comment says the max is "about 70 million in
                  2013"; the constructor asserts against Givaro's real
                  maxCardinality() (but is compiled out under -DNDEBUG); and
                  the existing tests already construct FFPACK rings at
                  33500479, 66000007 and 67108859, and pass.

  ARingZZpFlint   native 2^64-1, assumed < 2^63, enforced nowhere.
                  flint's nmod handles the full unsigned 64-bit range
                  (nmod.h:33-42 handles the high-bit-set case explicitly).
                  Neither ARingZZpFlint(size_t) nor rawARingZZpFlint validates
                  anything.
                  The reason for M2's < 2^63 assumption is the type chain at
                  the engine level, and only that: Ring::mCharacteristic is a
                  signed long (rings/ring.hpp:69, whose own comment admits
                  "not all rings will have characteristic that fits in a long
                  int"), rawRingCharacteristic returns long, and
                  ConcreteRing<ARingZZpFlint>::create feeds the ARing's
                  size_t characteristic straight into initialize_ring(long).
                  So a ring built at the largest prime below 2^64 reports a
                  characteristic of -59 through the generic Ring interface.
                  The ARing class itself is fine at that size.

                  NOTE, because it is easy to get this backwards:
                  coerceToLongInteger is *not* broken above 2^63.  It is total
                  for every p < 2^64.  The balanced representative always fits
                  in a long, since |rep| <= p/2 < 2^63.  The intermediate
                  "long result = f" does overflow when f > 2^63, but the
                  signed/unsigned conversions in "result > characteristic()/2"
                  and in "result -= characteristic()" round-trip on two's
                  complement, so the answer comes out right.  Verified
                  directly at p = 18446744073709551557 across both sides of
                  the p/2 boundary; see ARingZZpFlint.coerceToLongIntegerNear2to64.
                  (It does lean on implementation-defined conversions, which
                  is worth cleaning up, but it is not a wrong answer.)

                  What actually used to fail, and what the old
                  "this fails for charac > 2^63" comment on the
                  arithmetic18446744073709551557 test was really about, was
                  the *test helper*: testCoerceToLongInteger held the
                  characteristic in a long, so "characteristic() - 10000"
                  wrapped to -10059 once p exceeded 2^63 and the second loop
                  walked an entirely different range.  The helper now uses
                  unsigned arithmetic and reaches the top of the range through
                  the negative representatives, and the call is enabled again.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. Other observations
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Most of what this section first listed was fixed by the work described above:
  the assertion-free tests, the hand-rolled large-characteristic cases,
  testMultiply checking only a*0 == 0, the missing a^0 and EXPECT_THROW
  coverage, and the "commented out because" comments, which went away with the
  tests they sat on.  What remains:

  - ARingZZp::ARingZZp(size_t p0) stores p = static_cast<int>(p0).  Constructing
    the class directly, bypassing rawARingZZp's range check, with p0 > INT_MAX
    truncates silently.

  - ARingTest.hpp still annotates testAdd, testSubtract and testDivide as
    failing "in char 2, ffpack".  They do not; all three run at p=2 in the
    matrix.  Left in place rather than churn unrelated commentary.

  - Adjacent, outside ZZ/p scope: ARingTest.hpp and RingTest.hpp share the
    include guard M2_UNITTESTS__RING_TEST_HPP__ and define different values of
    ntrials (1000 vs 100).  testCoercions short-circuits on
    characteristic() == 0, skipping its entire rational half for exactly the
    characteristic-0 rings where rationals matter.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. Remaining work (postponed)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Stage 1 -- tests only, no engine changes -- is done and landed.  Everything
below is deliberately postponed, not in progress.  It is recorded so the next
person does not have to rediscover it.

Stage 2, engine fixes.  Each changes engine code and flips or updates a test
that already exists.  Roughly in order of payoff:

  S1  Replace ARingZZpFFPACK::getMaxModulus().  It returns a hardcoded 0x7fff
      behind "#if 1", with the real Givaro query stranded in the dead #else;
      Givaro's Modular<double>::maxCardinality() is 94906266.  Not cosmetic:
      interface/aring.cpp gates ring creation on it, so Strategy => "Ffpack"
      errors out above p = 32766 while the backend handles 94.9 million.
      Note the tripwire cuts both ways --
      ARingZZpFFPACK.advertisedMaxModulusIsBelowTheRealOne asserts
      getMaxModulus() < maxCardinality(), so this fix makes them equal and that
      assertion must become EXPECT_LE or be retired.  That is the
      characterization test doing its job.

  S2  Validate the flint modulus.  Neither ARingZZpFlint(size_t) nor
      rawARingZZpFlint checks anything, not primality and not range, while
      rawARingZZp enforces 2..32749.

  S3  Settle the 2^63 question.  This is not coerceToLongInteger, which is total
      for every p < 2^64 -- see section 5.  The constraint is
      Ring::mCharacteristic, a signed long fed from the ARing's size_t, so a
      ring at the largest prime below 2^64 reports a characteristic of -59
      through the generic Ring interface.  Enforce p < 2^63, or widen the type.

  S4  Make generator computation lazy.  ARingZZpFlint's constructor factors p-1
      on every ZZ/p creation and flint is the default strategy; ARingZZp runs an
      O(p^2) primitive-root search eagerly.  ARingZZpFFPACK is already lazy.

  S5  Guard the ARingZZp narrowing noted in section 6.  Flips
      ARingZZp.DISABLED_constructorRejectsModulusAboveIntMax.

  S6  Decide ARingZZp::subtract_multiple -- restore the zero check, assert in
      debug builds, or leave it to callers.  Flips
      ARingZZp.DISABLED_subtractMultipleByZero.

  S7  Minor: copy() is missing from ARingZZpFlint, so init_set is the only copy
      operation the three classes have in common.

Stage 3, coverage the ARing layer cannot reach.  promote, lift and eval take a
Ring* or a RingMap*, so they belong in Macaulay2/packages/EngineTests.m2, which
already drives the same ZZ/p strategies through the raw interface.  Reachable
but still untested: ARingZZp::syzygy, ARingZZp::random, ARingZZpFFPACK::field().

Stage 4, the two shared-harness defects in section 6.  Fixing the
characteristic-0 skip will surface real failures in the ZZ, QQ, RR and CC tests;
triage those as findings rather than silencing them.

Descoped by decision, recorded so it is not mistaken for an oversight: Galois
fields.  ARingGFTest.cpp is commented out of both build lists and does not
compile against the current API, so no GF(p^n) is under test anywhere;
ARingGFFlintBig and ARingGFM2 have never had a test file; and ARingGFM2::random
draws rawRandomInt(characteristic()) rather than the field order, so random
elements never leave the prime subfield.
